diff --git a/docs/node-report-freshness.md b/docs/node-report-freshness.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..5da5d52
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/node-report-freshness.md
@@ -0,0 +1,86 @@
+# Per-node report freshness
+
+The collector records separate receipt times for current block, stats and pending
+reports. A connected WebSocket, a newly saved database snapshot and a recently
+received application report describe different facts. None proves chain agreement,
+block advancement, valid ticket funding or successful automatic ticket purchases.
+
+## Receipt contract
+
+Each node's snapshot contains `reports: {block, stats, pending}` in UTC epoch
+milliseconds, or null when no accepted report exists in the current session.
+The collector supplies these times; client timestamps cannot set them. Valid
+repeated reports of unchanged values refresh the corresponding receipt. Partial
+or invalid reports do not. History replies, latency measurements and pings do not
+refresh these three current-report clocks. Reconnecting resets all three to null.
+
+The snapshot also projects `connected` from the collector's own connection state.
+It is independent of the node's self-reported `stats.active`. The stored receipt
+times and connection observation are facts; freshness labels and expiry budgets
+are derived on read. The existing JSONB document needs no schema migration or
+additional table. `block.received` remains the older propagation-cache timestamp;
+it is not used to date the current block report.
+
+Stats require boolean active/mining/syncing flags, a nonnegative integer peer
+count, uptime from 0 to 100, and nonnegative integer or unknown ticket counts.
+Current blocks require a valid height/hash and the existing transaction, uncle
+and difficulty fields; pending reports require a nonnegative integer count.
+This is validation of the report fields used here, not full blockchain validation
+or comprehensive telemetry resource validation. Combined legacy updates must
+contain all three groups. Their pending assignment and double-callback defect
+are corrected as part of making their receipt times reliable.
+
+Unknown own-ticket counts remain null, not zero or a numeric-looking placeholder.
+efsn's decimal-string latency is normalized to a number. Neither normalization
+changes a receipt clock. Older stored `N/A` ticket and string-latency values are
+also understood by the browser.
+
+## API and browser
+
+The API now requires `NODE_REPORT_MAX_AGE_MS`, a positive decimal integer from
+1 to 2147483647, independently of `SNAPSHOT_MAX_AGE_MS`. Choose the deployed
+value after measuring actual efsn reporting and snapshot latency. The reviewed
+efsn source schedules a full report every 15 seconds; test lifetimes are much
+shorter to exercise boundaries, and are not deployment recommendations.
+
+`/nodes` includes a derived `reportValidForMs` object for the three groups.
+Missing, invalid or future receipt metadata has a null budget. Disconnected or
+expired reports have zero. The API calculates remaining time using its clock;
+collector, snapshot writer and API must have synchronized clocks. Advancing the
+snapshot time alone never renews a receipt. `/blocks`, `/info` and `/charts`
+remain cached diagnostic views; their snapshot headers do not certify per-node
+report freshness. Consumers needing current reports must use `/nodes` metadata.
+
+The browser subtracts monotonic request elapsed time from each budget. A timer
+expires individual report groups even while another API request is pending.
+Expired fields become dashes, and the node remains listed with its connection
+status and Block/Stats/Pending labels: Recent, Stale, Not reported or Disconnected.
+Old snapshots without receipt metadata contribute no current values. Full
+snapshot expiry or API failure still removes the complete view and retries.
+Returning to a visible tab still forces a new request; suspended browser scripts
+cannot update the screen until execution resumes.
+
+Summaries use the connected node with the highest recent block report. Its
+pending count additionally requires its own recent pending report. Own tickets,
+mining, syncing, peers and reported uptime require recent stats. Last Latency is
+explicitly historical; no receipt age is available for that display. A green
+connection marker alone is not a report-health indicator.
+
+Deploy the collector, writer, API and browser candidate together. Configure the
+new API lifetime first. Metadata is additive; existing stored data fails closed
+for freshness until new accepted reports reach the snapshot. No stored balances,
+chain state, validator key or consensus rule is involved.
+
+## Local acceptance and remaining work
+
+132 contracts, nine real collector socket tests, eight React DOM tests, the
+strict production build, five PostgreSQL pipeline scenarios and 13 compiled
+Edge checkpoints pass. Tests include repeated equal reports, forged client
+receipt metadata, ping/history isolation, reconnect reset, independent expiry,
+pending-request expiry, unknown tickets and efsn latency encoding. The database
+suite checks actual stored receipt metadata through the HTTP API. Its outer
+test plus five scenarios produce six TAP passes.
+
+Chart-cache ordering/retention defects, outbound resource bounds, actual efsn/RPC
+comparison, production runtime/dependencies, supervision and public TLS hosting
+remain open. This is synthetic local acceptance, not public launch approval.
